  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_788baa97b5d5476984d103312af80006",
      "statement": "The standard test for return predictability is the predictive regression r_{t+1} = alpha + beta x_t + epsilon_{t+1}, with r_{t+1} the asset's excess return, x_t the predictor known at t, and null hypothesis beta = 0.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L18-L20",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b19d2ed4e98fe094bd56aa28cefe23d81225f4729e24ae1f856081e0247c1e28:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_67745eb9c3ea4b0ca5d9940f464fd5ac",
      "statement": "The out-of-sample R^2 is R^2_OS = 1 - sum(r_{t+1}-hat r_{t+1})^2 / sum(r_{t+1}-bar r_{t+1})^2, comparing the recursive model forecast against the historical mean; because estimating an extra coefficient injects noise, under the null the expected value of R^2_OS is slightly negative, so even a zero R^2_OS is weak evidence for predictability.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L21-L22",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:585183510515482084c067f486b1921b2315e80b49b99c77833f1ed2a98c45a4:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c105fbbf947b44eb97c81c219824eff8",
      "statement": "The yield-curve slope is the spread SPREAD_t = R^L_t - R^S_t, defined as the ten-year Treasury yield minus the three-month yield; a flat or inverted curve (SPREAD_t <= 0) tends to anticipate a real slowdown.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L28-L30",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7570411d2951e02b23e126d36285a4ef63e8e0771e86b5f8fcd289f437a77f3e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1b543879b11c48beab002d260b6b5e2c",
      "statement": "The benchmark four-quarter recession probit on 1960-1995 quarterly data is Pr(R_{t+4}=1) = Phi(-0.66 - 0.81 SPREAD_t) with pseudo-R^2 = 0.296 and t about -4.99; a spread of 0.76 points maps to a 10% recession probability, -0.82 to 50%, and -2.40 to 90%.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L34-L36",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fef575432bb67a71720cf9ee2fc29f4303dd8082e5dd251713491d11c97575ec:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ec263872da504fb9b0fd4b23cd520527",
      "statement": "Across leading indicators no single one dominates at every horizon, but the term spread is the clear choice beyond two quarters out to roughly four-to-six quarters, while equity indices are competitive at the very shortest horizons; adding a third regressor generally hurts.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L40-L40",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:75ca822a4457f6e3181dd94b82e76d730ed5817024c429bee9d1c8a6b6d7b9c7:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3d2bfac7ae224473a49a2d7d679a02da",
      "statement": "The term structure predicts continuous future real growth: with cumulative annualized real output growth Y_{t,t+k} = (400/k) log(y_{t+k}/y_t) regressed on the current spread on 1955-1988 quarterly data with Newey-West errors, the estimated slope is positive.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L44-L48",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:96ee9b65c28ae1c70772aa05765334569013a57097ed190d8b31bbf1f0c35728:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_090c7ebfd20042efbc64c0c14b4ea4ce",
      "statement": "At four quarters the real-growth regression gives alpha_0 = 1.70 and alpha_1 = 1.30, so a 100-basis-point spread predicts about 3% annual real growth; predictive power peaks at five-to-seven quarters where the spread alone explains more than a third of output variation (R^2-bar about 0.38), and the signal holds for private demand components but not government spending.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L50-L50",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:34344b0a20df6b8f7de347cb0a4e7f3a32f107fa11a1ecae447741953f92aeff:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f8ba2efa7ea94bf6945bb71d167bdfc1",
      "statement": "The GZ credit spread is built by discounting a corporate bond's exact cash flows on the zero-coupon Treasury curve to form a synthetic risk-free bond, giving S_{it}[k] = y_{it}[k] - y^f_t[k] and the index S^GZ_t = (1/N_t) sum_i sum_k S_{it}[k].",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L60-L61",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:509433f88340bd9299fb4ed8d3bffc4d1c69012b5920e1dafe56a94be0c33d7c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_42e40a775f4a41a9a36da5a1023f00cd",
      "statement": "Default risk is measured by a Merton-style distance-to-default DD_{it} = [ln(V_{it}/D_{it}) + (mu_{V,it} - 0.5 sigma^2_{V,it})] / sigma_{V,it} with implied default probability Phi(-DD), where the default point D is current liabilities plus half of long-term liabilities.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L62-L64",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a152fcc94afd926ac5cfe647ff2313383e9ead38564b700b2f0c265a45ff2769:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_52137a39c0bb47f7aa3825fb659de27e",
      "statement": "The excess bond premium is the residual EBP_t = S^GZ_t - hat S^GZ_t, where hat S^GZ_t is the expected-loss spread predicted by projecting the log spread on distance-to-default and issue characteristics (adjusted R^2 about 0.65-0.70).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L66-L70",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7885e671df261506232c2d5e31d6dc5a309693793cba7bfe0b143275e737f216:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3090a30b55d54503821540bb874ace5a",
      "statement": "The EBP predicts future consumption, investment, industrial production and employment with coefficients larger in absolute value than the expected-loss component; since the mid-1980s essentially all of the GZ spread's predictive content for output growth is attributable to the EBP, and a one-standard-deviation EBP shock (about 20 basis points) triggers a cumulative equity-market fall of roughly 7%.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L72-L72",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d140ea575de18290d74567b739f507237e0ffef34302c273767bd81e32efcf58:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_07496b38439f405cb01078da8b082515",
      "statement": "The volatility-managed portfolio scales the factor inversely to its expected variance, f^sigma_{t+1} = (c / hat sigma^2_t(f)) f_{t+1}, with c set so managed and original strategies share the same unconditional variance and hat sigma^2_t(f) = RV^2_t = sum_d f^2_{t,d} estimated from the prior month's daily realized variance.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L76-L78",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:27c765a9fec7822b0d9f635eff6ff030520888bed20aa58c20dacd02737185e5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f32df227b12a4decb9f6ff3cd9d967f5",
      "statement": "Regressing the managed strategy on the original yields positive significant alpha for the market (about 4.9% annual, beta about 0.6) and for value, momentum, profitability, investment and currency carry (size excepted); the appraisal ratio alpha/sigma(epsilon) is about 0.33 annual for the market, lifting Sharpe to S_new = sqrt(S_old^2 + (alpha/sigma_epsilon)^2), about a 25% increase, with mean-variance utility gain Delta U = (S_new^2 - S_old^2)/S_old^2 of about 65% for a market-only investor.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L80-L86",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0affa325f0cb280abd1db0af92724790f978a1983c6216f1d0a69a84e6e635bb:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c93683b638d84715b626da00847b8be7",
      "statement": "A panel of 132 macro series is compressed by principal components into r = 8 dynamic factors (first explaining 17.7% of panel variance, the eight together about 50%) via the factor structure x_{it} = lambda_i' f_t + e_{it}.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L92-L94",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7582adbdc50424b909d5e076ff19fe1e0b9d943fd70d3887be955ad23407024c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7646e08a7a4c428d808b559114a8c421",
      "statement": "Macro factors add bond-premium content beyond the Cochrane-Piazzesi factor: alone they explain 21%-26% of one-year excess-return variation, and with CP_t they raise R^2-bar to about 44% for the two-year bond versus 31% for CP_t alone; the resulting premia are strongly countercyclical, with the real factor correlating over 90% with industrial-production growth, and the result holds out of sample with RMSE 79%-93% of the constant-premium benchmark.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L96-L100",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:44f74806a729e921a84f7a304e90055b5e730fa7c3abb9e80290adabc07ad3df:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_dc27b3e68609445cb478e7dff0ddd495",
      "statement": "Combining fourteen macro-finance variables with fourteen technical trend rules via principal-component diffusion indices shows in-sample additivity: PC-ALL's R^2 (2.02%) equals the sum of PC-ECON (1.18%) and PC-TECH (0.84%), and PC-ALL reaches out-of-sample R^2_OS = 1.79%, above either family alone.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L104-L110",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d948a4dca33fd3ce0a03d92f7ad4007b3dc397838b61d9dbe4872445b1530b33:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_502e558f3b454e008d373cccf1313997",
      "statement": "The combined-forecast predictability is strongly countercyclical, with PC-ALL R^2_OS of 11.24% in recessions versus negative in expansions, and a mean-variance weight w_t = (1/gamma) hat r_{t+1} / hat sigma^2_{t+1} yields a certainty-equivalent gain of about 4.9% annual.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L112-L112",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:3e6fe1eac1a7d9f4a3581ed903217fe73e3dc8b91edacd82ec4422c0aaa6b28f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_432f4b974a834e86bf4544a885016e33",
      "statement": "A systematic exam of equity-premium predictors using the cumulative squared-error difference Delta_t = sum(r_s - bar r_{s-1})^2 - sum(r_s - hat r_s)^2 finds that for most variables Delta_t does not rise steadily: in-sample predictability does not survive out of sample, coefficients are unstable, much apparent success concentrates in a few episodes (the 1973-1975 oil shock), and out-of-sample R^2-bar is often negative.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L116-L118",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4ca0fe0407f76e928c628c9f48179d034d2b10a5f4cd8727d73304b144c65cfa:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1e6a6a602862481cbd6c154db6c2db42",
      "statement": "Theory-motivated restrictions make predictors useful: wrong-sign coefficients are set to zero and a negative premium forecast is truncated via hat r_t^restr = max(0, hat alpha_t + hat beta_t x_t), and a Gordon-growth valuation anchor builds the expected return as hat R_EP = (D/E)(E/P) + (1 - D/E) ROE; with these restrictions many predictors beat the historical mean out of sample with small but positive R^2_OS.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L120-L124",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:092ac7e54c382e2281abbc8f7babd54c2e36211ec768113f0ac6bcbf37b236f1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_20116c2e10df4b78886cc2baf3105b49",
      "statement": "Small out-of-sample R^2 is economically relevant because the proportional increase in a mean-variance investor's expected portfolio return is (R^2/(1-R^2))((1+S^2)/S^2), so the right yardstick for R^2 is the squared Sharpe S^2; with a monthly equity Sharpe near 0.11 (S^2 about 1.2%), a monthly R^2_OS near 0.43% raises portfolio return by about a third.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/29_macroeconomic_signals_and_expected_risk_premia.md#L126-L128",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:70ed4ac56e0e577e599ee66b22a2ff2c474b2dd1db653899d112eb9cf4b8e40a:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
